WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.740
Coding agents run real commands on your Mac.

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.100
Most are routine.

3
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.570
A few aren't.

4
00:00:07.164 --> 00:00:10.664
TraceRook checks every tool call before it runs.

5
00:00:10.684 --> 00:00:12.744
Routine work goes straight through.

6
00:00:12.764 --> 00:00:19.174
But when an agent tries to upload your SSH key,
a local rule blocks it before it executes.

7
00:00:19.693 --> 00:00:24.073
Clear-cut risks are decided on your Mac, in milliseconds.

8
00:00:24.093 --> 00:00:30.103
When an action is ambiguous, Claude weighs the
local signals against the task you gave your agent.

9
00:00:30.602 --> 00:00:37.942
When it's your call, TraceRook pauses the agent and
shows you the exact action, the evidence, and a deadline.

10
00:00:37.962 --> 00:00:41.972
Allow once releases that one call, and nothing more.

11
00:00:42.557 --> 00:00:47.527
Every session keeps its timeline
and evidence, in a native Mac app.

12
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:50.933
And your code stays on your Mac.

13
00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:55.843
Claude sees categories and signal
codes, never your commands or files.

14
00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:57.535
TraceRook.

15
00:00:57.555 --> 00:01:00.975
Let your agent build, and keep the next move in check.

16
00:01:00.995 --> 00:01:04.205
Request an invitation at tracerook.dev.
